  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using time series foundation models to generate probabilistic counterfactual forecasts, this study evaluated the mobility impacts of New York City's January 2025 congestion pricing program. The analysis of bus, subway, and aggregate trip volume data revealed a significant increase in post-policy transit ridership alongside a modest decrease in overall travel demand. These effects varied spatially, with overall demand reductions concentrated inside the Congestion Relief Zone, while public transit gains extended well beyond Manhattan's core.",
      "why": "It demonstrates a scalable, uncertainty-aware evaluation framework for large-scale urban interventions where traditional, clean control groups cannot be constructed due to system-wide spillovers."
    }
